  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "panels": [
      {
        "text": "## Arrow Functions.\nNow that you know how to write basic functions using the function keyword, let's look at a different way to write them, **arrow functions.**\n\nThis is a common format when working with event handlers or short, single-purpose functions.\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/04fc34f586edf1aed5925064e08ea304-arrowfunction.png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-e9a367e5-f514-45ee-8526-03828e8dfbfe",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## Comparing Syntax \n\n**Regular Function**\n\n- Defined using the function keyword\n\n**Arrow Function**\n\n- Stored in a variable\n\n👉 Even though they look different, they run the same code.\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/7d50e4079cbcb220b73cc7e02f8f0449-arrowfunction (1).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-b87ac174-1dd5-42c7-861a-225f5ef70be6",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## What is different \n\nIt’s a **variable** that stores a **function.**\n\nAn arrow function does not use  the `function` keyword.\nInstead, it creates a variable (using `const`) and assigns a function to it.",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/031c8d07af95561939d76e71d341f482-arrowfunction (2).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-1790152c-e777-4a70-b4cb-4f806a06a41e",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## The arrow defines the function\n\nIn an arrow function, the symbol `=>` introduces the function’s behavior.\n\nEverything after the arrow is the code that will run when the function is called.\n\nYou can think of the arrow as meaning:\n\n- “When this function runs, do this.”\n\nThe parentheses `() `still hold any inputs (parameters),\nbut the arrow separates the inputs from the instructions.",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/e2218759f8591bf9319855818c16e23e-arrowfunction (3).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-61549fa9-ed74-45b7-8aa1-5ccd009a6523",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## Semicolon ending\nBecause this is a variable assignment,\nwe end it with a semicolon, just like other variables.\n\n**Tip:** That’s a big structural difference students often miss.\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/9d720c3987a46adc4ad7160edb1d0ad6-arrowfunction (4).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-8eb18046-d43e-4e04-8f6c-00504b824640",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## Calling the Function\nEven though an arrow function is stored in a variable, you call it the same way as a regular function:\n\n- **addFive(); **✅ calls the function\n-  **addFive**  ✅ refers to the function (useful for event handlers)\n\n\n**Why would we use it without parentheses?**\n - Because sometimes we want to pass the function somewhere else (like an event listener), not run it immediately.\n\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/bbbd6304c9033f73f8ab8b9994f70c95-arrowfunction (5).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-e6af00dd-dd27-45df-b4c1-3e18abbaaa20",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "## Why Use Arrow Functions?\n- They are shorter.\n- They are common in modern JavaScript.\n- They are used heavily in frameworks and real-world code.\n- They make small, single-purpose functions less bulky.\n\nThey are mostly about:\n***Writing cleaner, more compact code.***\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/307afdb6724421a113f49e6f73ec3e9e-arrowfunction (6).png",
        "key": "pl-aif-2-U2-arrow-functions-panel-c5986024-3931-48e3-a07f-d8ca24d24764",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      }
    ],
    "name_suffix": "_2026",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false"
  },
